"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][17]={"school_physics_en:M17-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M17-L1 – Magnetic flux density and field models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Magnetic fields and charged-particle motion<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A compass needle changes direction near a wire carrying a current.\nWhat determines the new alignment, and how do we represent the magnetic\neffect?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>magnetic field<\/strong> is a physical field whose direction\nand strength are described by the vector <strong>magnetic flux\ndensity<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts SI unit is the <strong>tesla<\/strong> (T). The field exists even if\nno compass is placed inside it. A magnetic field line is an imaginary\nrepresentation: its tangent gives the local direction of the field, not\nnecessarily a particle trajectory. Magnetic field lines form closed\nloops. Outside a bar magnet they run from north to south and return\nwithin the magnet. No isolated magnetic poles have been observed.<\/p>\n<p>Around a long, straight conductor carrying a current, magnetic field\nlines form circles. For <strong>conventional current<\/strong>, the\nright-hand grip rule applies: point your thumb along the current; curled\nfingers indicate the direction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a long current-carrying solenoid the lines are approximately parallel\ninside and curve around outside. For an ideal, sufficiently long\nair-core solenoid,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\approx\\mu_0 nI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith turn density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not a universal expression for every real coil.<\/p>\n<p>Magnetic flux density must not be confused with magnetic flux\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich describes the field component passing through an area and is\nstudied in M18. The KMK Abitur standards place B in the common core of\nthe general and advanced levels. Equations for special conductor\ngeometries can be state-dependent extensions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_101_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Straight upward-current conductor with B emerging on the left and entering on the right; schematic long solenoid beside it.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Straight upward-current conductor with B\nemerging on the left and entering on the right; schematic long solenoid\nbeside it.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Field lines illustrate direction and structure, not exact\ncalibrated values.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A model uniform field has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.080<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.080\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nequal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>80<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80{,}000\\,\\mathrm{\\mu T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the current in an ideal, long air-core solenoid is doubled at\nconstant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nB doubles. Real coils do not necessarily follow this\nproportionality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe paper task:<\/strong> For an upward conventional current\nin a wire in the drawing plane, mark the field direction on either side\nusing dot-and-cross symbols. Then compare a schematic solenoid. Physical\ndemonstrations involving currents and magnets require supervised risk\nassessment; do not use strong magnets near medical implants.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI unit of magnetic flux density?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a tangent to a magnetic field line show?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the field-line pattern around a straight wire?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.025<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.025\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto millitesla.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Tesla (T).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The local direction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Circular loops around the wire, with direction given by the\nright-hand grip rule for conventional current.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>An infinitely long straight wire and long solenoid are idealized\ngeometries. The density of drawn lines is a convention; the diagram\nalone does not yield a calibrated field strength.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 20, §2.6.1: magnetic\nflux density and qualitative paths in the common core; quantitative\ntrajectories as an advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.2 Magnetic Fields and Lines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n11.2: magnetic field, Lorentz force, tesla and direction conventions ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-4-magnetic-force-on-a-current-carrying-conductor\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.4 Magnetic Force on a Current-Carrying\nConductor<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 11.4: circular field around a conductor and\nconventional current · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\n– Safe Schools, physics: working with electricity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electromagnetic\nfield precautions, medical implants and supervised electrical\nexperiments · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M17-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M17-L2 – Magnetic Lorentz force and direction rules<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Magnetic fields and charged-particle motion<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A positive charge travels to the right while the magnetic field\npoints upward. Where is the deflection, and how does the answer change\nfor an electron?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A moving charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a magnetic field experiences the magnetic force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F_{\\mathrm B}=q\\,\\vec v\\times\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts <strong>magnitude<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm B}=|q|vB\\sin\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angle between velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand flux density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe force is largest for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero for motion parallel or antiparallel to the field. A stationary\ncharge experiences no magnetic Lorentz force, although an electric force\nmight still exist.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>right-hand cross-product rule<\/strong> gives, for a\n<strong>positive<\/strong> charge moving along +x in a field along +y, a\nforce along +z (out of the page). For a <strong>negative<\/strong> charge\nthe force is reversed (−z, into the page). Conventional current\ndirection must not be confused with actual electron motion.<\/p>\n<p>Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F_{\\mathrm B}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis always perpendicular to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na purely magnetic field does no work on a point charge: it changes\ndirection but not speed. Qualitative forces and paths are part of the\nshared KMK Abitur core. Computing force magnitudes is a useful teaching\nstep; exactly which algebraic depth is compulsory depends on the federal\nstate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_102_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Positive and negative charges moving rightward in an upward field, experiencing opposite forces out of or into the page.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Positive and negative charges moving\nrightward in an upward field, experiencing opposite forces out of or\ninto the page.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Charge sign reverses the direction of q·(v×B).<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A particle of positive charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=3.2\\cdot10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmoves at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=4.0\\cdot10^5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nperpendicular to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.25\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=3.2\\cdot10^{-19}\\cdot4.0\\cdot10^5\\cdot0.25=3.2\\cdot10^{-14}\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt 30° the force is half as large because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin30^\\circ=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper direction exercise:<\/strong> Define x, y and z. First\ndraw\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong +x and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong +y; find the force for a proton and then for an electron. A dot ⊙\nindicates out of the page, and a cross ⊗ indicates into the page.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When does the magnetic Lorentz force vanish?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong +x and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong +y, what is the force direction for positive charge?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the example force at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a magnetic field alone not alter kinetic energy?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When the particle is stationary or the velocity is\nparallel/antiparallel to B.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Along +z, out of the page.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.6\\cdot10^{-14}\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Magnetic force is perpendicular to velocity and therefore does no\nwork.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The vector equation is a classical point-charge model. At very high\nspeed or with additional electric fields, a broader description is\nrequired.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 20, §2.6.1: magnetic\nflux density and qualitative paths in the common core; quantitative\ntrajectories as an advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.2 Magnetic Fields and Lines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n11.2: magnetic field, Lorentz force, tesla and direction conventions ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.3 Motion of a Charged Particle in a Magnetic\nField<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 11.3: circular motion, helical paths, radius and\nperiod in a uniform B field · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M17-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M17-L3 – Paths of charged particles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Magnetic fields and charged-particle motion<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How can a particle move in a circle inside a magnetic field if the\nforce never acts tangentially to its velocity?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In a uniform, time-independent magnetic field, the path depends on\nthe angle between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the particle starts <strong>parallel<\/strong> to the field, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F_{\\mathrm B}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand it continues in uniform straight-line motion in the ideal model. If\nvelocity is <strong>perpendicular<\/strong> to the field, the magnetic\nforce is perpendicular to velocity and provides the <strong>centripetal\nforce<\/strong> required for circular motion. Speed remains constant in a\nconstant magnetic field, although the direction of velocity continually\nchanges.<\/p>\n<p>For an <strong>oblique entry angle<\/strong>, split velocity into\nparallel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\parallel}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand perpendicular\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\perp}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomponents. The parallel component persists, whereas the perpendicular\ncomponent generates circular motion. The result is a <strong>helical\ntrajectory<\/strong>. Reversing the charge sign reverses curvature\ndirection for otherwise identical starting conditions.<\/p>\n<p>A field line is not a particle’s circular path. In a uniform field,\nfield lines run parallel to B; circular orbits lie in planes\nperpendicular to B. The qualitative distinction among paths belongs to\nthe common KMK Abitur core. Calculating the orbital radius is deferred\nto M17-L6 as additional advanced-level material.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_103_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three panels illustrate idealised straight, circular and helical paths for differing angles between velocity and B.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three panels illustrate idealised\nstraight, circular and helical paths for differing angles between\nvelocity and B.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Perpendicular velocity produces circular motion; parallel\nvelocity produces helix advance.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A positive particle initially travels rightward (+x) into a field\ndirected <strong>into the page<\/strong> (−z). Its initial force is\nupward (+y), so its trajectory curves anticlockwise. A negative particle\nat the same initial velocity curves the other way.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Schematic matching task:<\/strong> For B along +z, draw three\ncases: (A) velocity +z, (B) velocity +x, (C) velocity with both x and z\ncomponents. Match straight line, circle and helix. Label every drawing\n“ideal uniform B field”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What path occurs if velocity is parallel to B?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What force provides the centripetal acceleration?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which path results if both parallel and perpendicular velocity\ncomponents are nonzero?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are magnetic field lines not the circular particle\ntrajectories?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Uniform straight-line motion if no other forces act.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The magnetic Lorentz force.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A helix.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Field lines show the direction of B; the orbit lies in a plane\nperpendicular to B.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Collisions, nonuniform fields, electric forces and energy losses are\nomitted. Actual tracks are not always perfect circles or helices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 20, §2.6.1: magnetic\nflux density and qualitative paths in the common core; quantitative\ntrajectories as an advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.3 Motion of a Charged Particle in a Magnetic\nField<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 11.3: circular motion, helical paths, radius and\nperiod in a uniform B field · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M17-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M17-L4 – Energy and deflection in an electric field<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Magnetic fields and charged-particle motion<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A positively charged particle moves from a higher-potential plate\ntoward a lower-potential plate. Does it gain speed, and why is this\nunlike a purely magnetic force?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In an <strong>electric field<\/strong>, a charge q experiences\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F_{\\mathrm E}=q\\vec E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis force can act even on a stationary charge and, unlike a purely\nmagnetic force, can have a component along the motion. Between points A\nand B the change in electric potential energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=q(\\varphi_B-\\varphi_A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf no other work or losses occur, energy conservation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{kin}}=q(\\varphi_A-\\varphi_B)=qU_{AB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{AB}=\\varphi_A-\\varphi_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For a <strong>positive<\/strong> charge, travelling from high to low\npotential increases kinetic energy. A <strong>negative<\/strong> charge\naccelerates against the field direction and gains speed toward higher\npotential. The magnitude of the energy change depends on charge and\npotential difference, not directly on mass. An electron-volt is an\nenergy unit:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{eV}=1.602\\cdot10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>If a particle enters horizontally between large horizontal plates,\nwhile the uniform electric field points vertically, its vertical\nacceleration is approximately constant and its trajectory is\n<strong>parabolic<\/strong> within the field in a classical\napproximation. Fringing fields modify the motion near plate edges. Under\nthe KMK standards, energy changes in uniform electric fields are shared\ncore material; quantitative path analysis is an advanced-level\naddition.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_104_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two horizontal plates, positive above and negative below, downward electric-field arrows and a downward-curving positive-charge trajectory.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two horizontal plates, positive above and\nnegative below, downward electric-field arrows and a downward-curving\npositive-charge trajectory.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Electric force can change kinetic energy; the plotted trajectory\nis idealised.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A positive charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=2.0\\,\\mathrm{nC}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntraverses a potential drop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{AB}=50\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nElectric work is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm E}=qU_{AB}=2.0\\cdot10^{-9}\\cdot50=1.0\\cdot10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm J=0.10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout losses, the particle gains this amount of kinetic energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Drawing and energy accounting:<\/strong> Label horizontal\nplates + on top and − below, draw the field downward, and determine the\ninitial force on a positive particle entering horizontally. Keep any\nquantitative parabola equation clearly marked advanced. Do not construct\na high-voltage deflection apparatus.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do electric and purely magnetic forces differ in their ability\nto do work?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI unit of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">qU<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the energy gain for positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1\\,\\mathrm{nC}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacross\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{AB}=20\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does an electron accelerate relative to the electric field?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Electric forces may do work; magnetic force on a point charge cannot\ndo work by itself.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Joules (J), since C · V = J.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\cdot10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm J=20\\,\\mathrm{nJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Opposite to the electric-field direction if no other force\ndominates.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Uniform field, ignored fringe effects and nonrelativistic motion.\nCollisions, radiation and other forces may change energy transfer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 20, §2.6.1: magnetic\nflux density and qualitative paths in the common core; quantitative\ntrajectories as an advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-2-electric-potential-and-potential-difference\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 7.2 Electric Potential and Potential\nDifference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 7.2: electric potential energy, potential\ndifference and electron-volt · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.3 Motion of a Charged Particle in a Magnetic\nField<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 11.3: circular motion, helical paths, radius and\nperiod in a uniform B field · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\n– Safe Schools, physics: working with electricity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electromagnetic\nfield precautions, medical implants and supervised electrical\nexperiments · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M17-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M17-L5 – Experiments with charged particles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Magnetic fields and charged-particle motion<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A teaching simulation displays a glowing ring whose radius changes\nwhen magnetic flux density is varied. What must remain constant to make\nthe comparison meaningful?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Start with a <strong>testable question<\/strong>: How does orbit\nradius r depend on magnetic field B? With particle species and speed\nunchanged, a plausible hypothesis is that stronger B produces a smaller\norbit radius. A fair test keeps particle identity, charge magnitude,\ninitial speed, field orientation and geometry as constant as possible. B\nis the independent variable; r is measured.<\/p>\n<p>A traditional classroom demonstration is the <strong>fine-beam\nelectron tube<\/strong>: electrons move inside a low-pressure gas tube;\nthe visible glowing trace results from gas excitation and collisions,\nnot from electrons being directly visible. Actual equipment may involve\nhigh voltage, glass and magnetic fields. <strong>This course does not\nprovide assembly or operating instructions.<\/strong> For independent\nstudy, use supplied data or an approved simulation instead.<\/p>\n<p>In an analysis, record at least five points where available, give r\nin metres, estimate uncertainty if warranted and plot r versus B. In the\nquantitative model at fixed speed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\propto1/B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\propto B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf acceleration voltage U is changed instead, both v and r generally\nchange, requiring a different comparison. Charge sign affects the\ndirection of curvature, not the magnitude of the orbit radius. Equipment\napproval remains a teacher responsibility.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_105_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Plot of model pairs B in mT and r in cm, plus controlled variables: particle type, speed and field geometry.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Plot of model pairs B in mT and r in cm,\nplus controlled variables: particle type, speed and field\ngeometry.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At fixed m, q and v, r is inversely proportional to B; values are\nsimulated.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A synthetic simulation series uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(B,r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.0\\,\\mathrm{mT},4.0\\,\\mathrm{cm})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.0\\,\\mathrm{mT},2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4.0\\,\\mathrm{mT},1.0\\,\\mathrm{cm})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn each case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Br=4.0\\,\\mathrm{mT\\,cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is <strong>model-generated data<\/strong>, not an actual laboratory\nrecord.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Data analysis without apparatus:<\/strong> Tabulate B and r\nfrom the example, plot the points and discuss why plotting r against 1/B\nwould yield a straight line. List two real sources of uncertainty, such\nas nonuniform fields and imprecise reading of the ring diameter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which variable is deliberately changed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What must be held fixed when investigating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What radius does the model predict at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=8\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the glowing track not a direct picture of individual\nelectrons?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Magnetic flux density B.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At least particle species, charge magnitude, speed and\ngeometry.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.5\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the stated model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The light comes from interactions of electrons with the gas in the\ntube.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The data are synthetic and idealized. Real experiments involve\nscatter, fringe fields, finite resolution and supervised safety\nassessment. No unsupervised high-voltage work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 20, §2.6.1: magnetic\nflux density and qualitative paths in the common core; quantitative\ntrajectories as an advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.3 Motion of a Charged Particle in a Magnetic\nField<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 11.3: circular motion, helical paths, radius and\nperiod in a uniform B field · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\n– Safe Schools, physics: working with electricity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electromagnetic\nfield precautions, medical implants and supervised electrical\nexperiments · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M17-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M17-L6 – Advanced level: quantitative orbit analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Magnetic fields and charged-particle motion<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How does an electron orbit change if magnetic flux density is\ndoubled? We now derive a quantitative result from the qualitative\npattern of M17-L5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Additional advanced-level content:<\/strong> A particle of\nmass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncharge magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nenters perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field B. Magnetic force has\nmagnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|vB<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the required centripetal force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mv^2/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEquating them yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|vB=\\frac{mv^2}{r}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad r=\\frac{mv}{|q|B}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The angular frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=|q|B/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the period is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi m/(|q|B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor fixed particle properties and speed, doubling B halves the radius,\nbut does not change speed. If a particle is accelerated from rest\nthrough a potential difference with the appropriate sign, the classical\nenergy equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12 mv^2=|q|\\,|U|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{2|q|\\,|U|/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore, for perpendicular entry,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\propto\\sqrt{|U|}/B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDoubling acceleration voltage multiplies r by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot by two.<\/p>\n<p>These are not universal orbit equations. They assume a uniform\nmagnetic field only, perpendicular entry, nonrelativistic motion and\nnegligible collisions. For oblique entry, only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\perp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsets the helix radius; the parallel part produces axial travel. Under\nKMK Abitur standards, <strong>quantitative path analysis<\/strong> is\nexpressly additional advanced-level content.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_106_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Calculated electron orbit radius in mm against B in mT, with labelled example points.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Calculated electron orbit radius in mm\nagainst B in mT, with labelled example points.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>With m, q and speed fixed, doubling B halves orbital\nradius.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For an electron with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.11<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_e=9.11\\cdot10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|e|=1.602\\cdot10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2.0\\cdot10^6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=2.0\\,\\mathrm{mT}=2.0\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.69<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.69<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=mv/(|q|B)\\approx5.69\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm m=5.69\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe period is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.79<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi m/(|q|B)\\approx1.79\\cdot10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm s=17.9\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDoubling B reduces the radius to about 2.84 mm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Calculation and graph:<\/strong> Compute model radii for B =\n1, 2 and 4 mT at constant particle type and speed. Plot r against 1/B\nand explain the straight line through the origin. Identify why the\nformula needs revision at very high energy (relativistic effects).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive the radius equation from two force expressions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does r change when B doubles, all else constant?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does r depend on acceleration voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|U|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom rest?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the example radius at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=4.0\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|vB=mv^2/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand solve for r.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The radius halves.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\propto\\sqrt{|U|}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed B, m and charge magnitude.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.84<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\approx2.84\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Classical nonrelativistic motion, uniform B and no electric-field\ncontribution inside the orbit. Radiation, collisions, space charge and\nmaterial interactions are omitted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L1 – Magnetic Flux and the\nInduction Principle<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 20, §2.6.1: magnetic\nflux density and qualitative paths in the common core; quantitative\ntrajectories as an advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.3 Motion of a Charged Particle in a Magnetic\nField<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 11.3: circular motion, helical paths, radius and\nperiod in a uniform B field · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 2, 11.2 Magnetic Fields and Lines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n11.2: magnetic field, Lorentz force, tesla and direction conventions ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
